Draw the **Fathom trench tileset**, a 19-frame **sprite sheet** of pac-man-style
rounded rock walls and the corridor floor, fog, and den gate, using only the
drawing tool.

*Fathom* is a bioluminescent deep-sea maze chase played on a tile-locked maze of
flooded rock corridors. You are drawing the **walls** of that maze (the renderer
picks the right wall tile for each board cell from its neighbors — an autotile),
plus the corridor floor, the unrevealed fog, and the den gate. These are **tiles,
not sprites**: each one fills its whole 32×32 cell, edge to edge, to butt
seamlessly against its neighbors.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`.
You build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a time**, each targeting a
frame with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you issue are recorded to a
per-frame action log, and that log is your submission.

Each frame is its **own separate 32×32 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-rect --frame 5 --x 10 --y 0 --width 12 --height 32 --color "#16293d"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the tileset up operation by operation until each `frames/<index>.png`
matches the brief: a consistent 16-piece autotile of rounded, raised rock walls
(frames 0–15, the index being the connection bitmask) whose connected edges line
up seamlessly, plus the seamless open-water floor (16), the flat unrevealed fog
(17), and the den gate (18), all in the trench palette. Settle one wall
cross-section and reuse its exact thickness and rim in every tile so they tile
without seams. When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-frame action logs
are your finished submission.
